<!-- Server-side memory ceiling for ClickHouse. Mounted by default in both
     compose files; there is nothing to copy or uncomment.

     It used to ship as `memory.xml.example`, opt-in. That is how a production
     instance ran for months with no ceiling at all: the airgapped compose (the
     one that actually reaches production) carried neither a container limit
     nor this mount, so ClickHouse derived its own ceiling from
     max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio (0.9) times what it believed total
     RAM to be. In a container with no memory limit that is the whole host —
     64 GiB, shared with Postgres, Qdrant and the app — so it grew to ~57 GiB
     and the kernel SIGKILLed it: `clickhouse-1 exited with code 137`, with no
     ClickHouse-side memory error anywhere in its log, and `restart:
     unless-stopped` quietly bringing it back. A guardrail nobody enables is
     not a guardrail.

     Why this exists as well as the container's mem_limit: mem_limit only
     decides who the kernel kills. This decides whether ClickHouse throttles
     itself before it gets there — the difference between one query failing
     with a clean memory error and the whole server dying.

     Sizing: leave real headroom under the container limit. This setting bounds
     what ClickHouse *accounts for*; jemalloc slack, mmapped pages and thread
     stacks sit outside that accounting. The reference stack pairs
     `mem_limit: 12g` with the 9.5 GiB below. Scale both together — the app's
     scan budget is derived from whatever ClickHouse reports here, so raising
     one number is enough to move the whole stack, and `/api/health`'s
     `scan_budget` block reports what actually resolved.

     Keep the pinned value under (ratio x mem_limit), which is 9.6 GiB here.
     Above it ClickHouse clamps silently (see the ratio note below) and the
     pinned number stops being the real ceiling — the app models that clamp
     when it reads this file's values back off the server, so the two agree,
     but a config whose stated limit is not its effective one is a trap for
     whoever reads it next.

     See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md "Resource sizing" for a worked 64 GiB example. -->
<clickhouse>
  <!-- 9.5 GiB, paired with `mem_limit: 12g` — under the 9.6 GiB the ratio
       below implies, so this value is adopted as written rather than clamped. -->
  <max_server_memory_usage>10200547328</max_server_memory_usage>

  <!-- The effective ceiling is the *lower* of the absolute value above and
       (this ratio x detected RAM) — ClickHouse clamps the absolute value down
       and logs a "lowered to" line when it does. So on a box where the server
       under-detects RAM, or if this file is copied onto a smaller node, the
       real limit may be under what is pinned above; check the server log
       before trusting the pinned number. Vestigo's own probe applies the same
       clamp when it derives the scan budget, so `/api/health`'s `scan_budget`
       reports the effective ceiling rather than the pinned one. Spelled out rather than left at the
       default so an operator who clears max_server_memory_usage doesn't
       silently fall back to 0.9 of a possibly-misdetected total. -->
  <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>0.8</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>

  <!-- Caches count against the ceiling above, so their defaults spend the
       budget on cache instead of on queries. At 26.6 defaults that is not a
       trim but a rout: mark 2 GiB + index-mark 5 GiB + primary-index 5 GiB =
       12 GiB of cache maxima under a 9.5 GiB ceiling, i.e. the ceiling is
       already exceeded before one query is admitted. Vestigo's own budget is
       now taken of (ceiling - these), and /api/health reports the figure, so
       leaving one at its default visibly shrinks the scan budget instead of
       silently overcommitting the server.

       The uncompressed caches are off. Stated rather than left at the default
       so an upgrade cannot turn them on underneath this budget. -->
  <mark_cache_size>2147483648</mark_cache_size>
  <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>
  <!-- 512 MiB / 1 GiB against 5 GiB each by default. Vestigo's scans are
       whole-corpus GROUP BYs over a handful of columns: the marks and the
       primary index for the parts in flight, not a working set that rewards a
       5 GiB cache. -->
  <index_mark_cache_size>536870912</index_mark_cache_size>
  <index_uncompressed_cache_size>0</index_uncompressed_cache_size>
  <primary_index_cache_size>1073741824</primary_index_cache_size>
</clickhouse>
